• access to education and childcare;
• food security;
• health services (for example, access to or affordability of health care);
• housing;
• health and sanitation (for example, drinking water access and quality; latrines; access to Personal Protective Equipment on Small Farms; or community health concerns such as high levels of HIV infection, diabetes, infant mortality, maternal death rate); and,
• for small producers: environmental health, farm productivity, and product quality (for example, climate adaptation and mitigation, water conservation, maintaining soil quality, and planting materials). 
Additional recommended themes include:
• community infrastructure (for example, roads, bridges, or community buildings);
• community services (for example, access to trainings or other support for livelihoods improvements);
• transportation (for example, access to transit to and from work, grocery shopping and other necessities, and recreational purposes);
• gender equity, including participation by women in income generating activities and farm decision-making, and other women’s empowerment issues; 
• provision of trainings;
• access to language training as a type of education (for farmers and workers);
• compliance and certification costs on Small Farms; and
• cooperative business needs.
It is best practice, but not required, that the Premium Management Team (PMT) be involved in planning and undertaking the Needs Assessment in the first year. Note that in 1.2.1.d, by Year Six the PMT will need to take an active role in updating the Needs Assessment.
It is recommended that a summary of the Needs Assessment results also be shared with all Premium Participants.

Coffee (Green Coffee Platform), Cocoa, and Palm Amendment: For coffee, the Needs Assessment shall include an evaluation of the following areas:
• environmental health, farm productivity, and product quality including climate adaptation and mitigation and quality parameters of coffee beans;
•  gender equity, including participation by women in income generating activities and farm decision-making, and other women’s empowerment issues; and
• provision of trainings.
See section 1.1 of this document for changes in applicability.
For coffee producers, this criteria applies to all farms and facilities size category.

